        {
          "id": "the_energy_spine_is_running_exhaust_alone_and_reproduces_the_generator_table",
          "description": "Two claims, and they are checked together because the second is the evidence for the first.\n\n`rtDay_meanBaseRate` on the 9101 rows IS `baserate_1_2020.meanBaseRate`, the reference's own S14 intermediate, computed here from `driveschedulesecond`'s 63,602 speeds. That is the same check `mixed-onroad` and `process-brakewear` make, at the same hour, and it is what says the energy this fixture chains off is right before any refueling arithmetic touches it.\n\nAND IT IS EXACTLY ZERO ON THE OTHER TWO ENERGY POLLUTANT-PROCESSES. 9102 has 12,160 `emissionrate` rows and 104 of the run's cohorts match one of its source bins (`pp_rateCohortCount`), so the rate lookup is not what stops it: its rates are on operating modes 101-108, extended idle's on mode 200, and `dc_W` is the drive-cycle distribution over the 23 RUNNING modes of an on-network road type, so the contraction has no partner. In the reference the same rows are discarded one step earlier, by `BaseRateCalculator`'s join to `runSpecRoadType` -- start rates are emitted off-network and this run selects road type 4 alone (`run_offNetworkRoadTypeCount` = 0). The two mechanisms agree HERE because no off-network road type is selected; on a run that selected one they would not, and this test and that count are what would fail.\n\n`pp_energyTotal` sums the whole spine so the zero is not a sampled one: 3.3254e+08 kilojoules on 9101 against exactly 0 on 9102 and 9190.\n\n`rtDay_meanBaseRateACAdj` is the incremental A/C load of sbweighted.rs:459. It reaches nothing -- the A/C factor is exactly 0 at this heat index -- but the generator table carries it in its own column, so it is checked here rather than left uncomputed. The tolerance is the reference's six-significant-figure column storage.",
